----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, N: g8-f6, N: g8-h6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: This is the starting position of a chess game but with a white knight already developed to c3 (NWF1 at c3). As Black, we need to respond appropriately. The white knight on c3 controls central squares (d5 and b5) and supports White's central pawn advance. Valid moves are standard Black opening moves: pawn pushes to 5 or 6, knights developing.  \nPromising moves:  \n1. **P: d7-d5** – immediately challenges the center, opens lines for bishop and queen, and is a classical response.  \n2. **P: e7-e5** – also fights for central control, though the knight on c3 indirectly eyes d5.  \n3. **N: g8-f6** – develops a knight toward the center, prepares kingside castling.  \n\nReason:  \n- d7-d5 is solid and directly contests central squares. It prevents White from easily playing e2-e4 without confrontation, and it opens up the c8 bishop.  \n- e7-e5 allows Black to establish a pawn center but leaves d5 weak to the knight on c3; White could later play d4 or even Nb5.  \n- g8-f6 develops a piece but does not address central pawn tension immediately.  \n\nGiven that White has already developed a knight, it's wise to claim central space with a pawn. Between d5 and e5, d5 is more flexible (can later support e5 if needed) and less directly challenged by the knight.  \n\nAnswer: P: d7-d5"}
